<p>For the last six months or so, I have been <a href="https://joshblais.com/blog/switching-to-hugo/">using Hugo</a> as my blogging platform of choice, switching over from a heavy NextJS site to one that is far more lightweight and focused on static files.</p>
<p>What I did not know is that <a href="https://gohugo.io/content-management/front-matter/#emacs-org-mode">Hugo plays very nicely</a> with <a href="https://orgmode.org/">org-mode</a>, so much so that all I have to do is change up the way my front-matter appears and name the post “post.org” instead of “post.md”, and Hugo takes care of the rest.</p>
<p>This has increase quality of life so much that I now just write in my blog directory (in drafts), and when a post is ready to go, I change the <code>#+DRAFT</code> flag from true to false, and the post goes live.</p>
<p>Now, my entire writing workflow is contained in org-mode, backed up to source control, and released on a schedule that is manageable. I have <strong>hundreds</strong> of unreleased blog that will be going out over 2025, so go <a href="https://joshblais.com/index.xml">subscribe to my RSS feed</a> so you don’t miss it!</p>
<p>The goal is to release 100 posts (I <a href="https://joshblais.com/blog/">released 48</a> in 2024) and 100 videos this year, which is aspirational, but what is life without aspiration?</p>
<p>God bless, and until next time.</p>